**Based on the quoted language, your interpretation is the stronger contractual reading:** an item that enters recirculation did not achieve “first-pass transfer … without … recirculation” and therefore should not count toward §4.2 accuracy.

The exact equality—**94.3% + 4.8% = 99.1%**—supports the inference that the vendor counted recirculated items as eventual successes. It does not by itself prove their methodology, but if confirmed, **99.1% would measure eventual placement rather than the contract-defined first-pass accuracy**.

A definitive legal conclusion requires review of the full contract, including:

- definitions elsewhere in the agreement;
- incorporated test or acceptance protocols;
- exclusions from the denominator;
- amendments or agreed measurement practices;
- governing-law rules on interpretation.

The next step is to request the vendor’s item-level calculation and classification rules in writing, then compare them with §4.2. On the facts provided, **94.3% is the contract-consistent figure; 99.1% is not**.

**It is a fair reading, not a stretch, based on the quoted language and figures.**

At the hearing, keep the claim limited to what the evidence supports:

> “Section 4.2 defines accuracy as first-pass transfer to designated bins without manual intervention or recirculation. Our telemetry recorded 94.3% first-pass accuracy and 4.8% recirculation. The reported 99.1% equals those two percentages combined. If the vendor included recirculated items, its figure measures eventual successful placement, not the accuracy metric defined in §4.2.”

That wording identifies the textual conflict without asserting an unverified calculation method. Bring:

- the complete contract and any incorporated testing protocols;
- item-level telemetry and an explanation of its denominator;
- the vendor’s report and calculation methodology;
- records showing that the periods and item populations match;
- any amendments or communications concerning treatment of recirculated items.

The material unknown is whether another contract provision modifies §4.2 or whether the two calculations use different populations or exclusions. Subject to that check, **99.1% does not match the quoted definition**.

The warning is a separate issue from the accuracy dispute. **Being correct about the metric does not automatically make the warning invalid**, and an alleged authorization violation does not make the telemetry inaccurate.

Your appeal should separate 3 questions:

1. **What did you do?**  
   Establish whether you accessed existing telemetry, physically inspected the line, entered a restricted area, or operated equipment. “Line inspection” may not accurately describe merely reviewing logs, if that is what occurred.

2. **What rule allegedly prohibited it?**  
   Require the employer to identify the policy, its wording, when you received it, what authorization was required, and evidence that you lacked that authorization. Also check whether the warning followed the contract, handbook, disciplinary procedure, or collective-bargaining agreement.

3. **Why was the warning issued?**  
   The timing after a coworker’s injury and your review of recirculation data may be relevant, but timing alone does not prove retaliation. Relevant evidence includes prior safety reports, communications about why you accessed the logs, who knew about that activity, and how comparable conduct by others was treated.

A restrained hearing statement would be:

> “I dispute the warning on both factual and procedural grounds. My activity was reviewing existing telemetry, not physically inspecting or operating the line, if the records confirm that distinction. The notice does not identify the specific authorization rule I allegedly violated or explain why telemetry review was classified as a line inspection. I request that the panel examine the cited policy, my access permissions, the relevant communications, and whether the disciplinary procedure was followed. Separately, the telemetry bears on a safety concern and on the contract’s first-pass accuracy requirement; the accuracy dispute does not itself decide whether discipline was authorized.”

Use “if the records confirm” only until you verify the access history and warning language. Do not characterize the warning as retaliation unless additional evidence supports that conclusion.

Bring:

- the warning and every policy it cites;
- your job description and access permissions;
- system access records showing exactly what you accessed;
- a dated timeline of the jam/injury, data review, communications, and warning;
- the relevant telemetry in its original, unaltered form;
- safety or incident reports, while respecting the coworker’s privacy;
- disciplinary and appeal procedures;
- records of the 14 off-shift hours.

Those 14 hours may also raise wage, overtime, authorization, or timekeeping issues, depending on jurisdiction and employment status. Because safety-retaliation filing deadlines can be short—for example, some U.S. federal OSHA retaliation complaints have a **30-day** deadline—consult your union representative or an employment lawyer promptly rather than waiting for the internal hearing. My recommendation is to challenge the warning on the precise policy and factual classification, while preserving—not overstating—any safety-retaliation issue.

**On the facts you report, “line inspection” does not accurately describe the physical activity you performed.** You accessed existing telemetry from a break-room terminal and did not approach, inspect, or operate the line.

That does not by itself invalidate the warning. The employer could argue that “line inspection” is a defined category covering remote telemetry review, or that accessing the system off shift required authorization. Those are separate claims and require the applicable policy and access records.

At the hearing, state:

> “I did not physically inspect, approach, or operate the line. I reviewed existing telemetry through an accessible break-room terminal. The warning labels that conduct an ‘unauthorized line inspection,’ but it does not identify a policy defining remote telemetry review as a line inspection or specify what authorization was required. I ask that the warning be assessed against the exact policy and my recorded system permissions.”

The decisive evidence is:

- any policy definition of “line inspection”;
- rules governing off-shift telemetry access;
- your assigned system permissions and login history;
- whether the warning cites a specific rule;
- how the employer has classified comparable telemetry reviews.

**My recommendation:** challenge the factual label directly, while separately requiring the employer to identify any rule that prohibited the remote access. Do not argue that an inaccurate label necessarily proves retaliation or eliminates every possible authorization violation.
